  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2022-03-10T19:04:54.313Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Hi, I'm Furriest. I am federally underage, and have been on the platform for a long time now 2-3+ years, and throughout that time I have been subject to lots of harassment, and inappropriate behaviors and interactions from adult figures that, due to the anonymity of VRChat by nature, cannot be moderated/policed effectively due to the fact that VRChat's moderation system does not handle private instances very well among small groups of individuals, and that VRChat is still in development. \nHosted among communities of these people were a very rare dozen malicious individuals who corrupted my views of sexuality among other things when I just-entered the platform. Due to the nature of these encounters on this platform, I have had a-lot more struggles on the emotional and academic environment because being taken advantage of is really painful.\nFor these reasons, I ask that there be effective algorithms in-place that better separate age-groups to prevent the behaviors I've been subjected to, manipulated into thinking for a time to be acceptable, and have experienced from older-aged users. Hopefully, this system is able to prevent the cultural environments that cause these issues, and is secure or  hard to bypass by users, comparable to the trust system.\nIn summary, VRChat should be able to detect users who use and create inappropriate avatars via a moderation system that can properly flag NSFW avatars that are not labeled as NSFW via the \"report avatar as inappropriate\" feature, and algorithmically blacklist a good-majority of users from seeing them, along with ensuring that VRChat does its best to keep instances separated by its age-groups for these flagged users.\nIf a system cannot be made to attempt separate individuals by age group (when an instance is being made and when players join public instances), a better system would be to have an automatically enabled setting that disables all inappropriate avatars from being seen by these users (mostly quest users), along with harsher moderation for the creators who did not label these avatars as such.",
